# April – June.
DATA = [
('04-02', 'Saint Francis of Paola, hermit', 'Saint Francis of Paola', '1416, Paola, Italy', '1507, Tours, France', ['sailors'],
 'Francis lived as a hermit from his teens and drew many followers by his holiness. He founded the Minim Friars, who lived in great humility and perpetual fasting.\n\nKnown for miracles, he was called to France to attend the dying King Louis XI and spent his last years there.'),
('04-04', 'Saint Isidore, bishop and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Isidore of Seville', 'c. 560, Spain', '636, Seville, Spain', ['students', 'the internet'],
 'Isidore succeeded his brother as Archbishop of Seville. A great scholar, he wrote an encyclopaedia, the *Etymologies*, that preserved learning for centuries, and worked for the unity and education of the Church in Spain.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('04-05', 'Saint Vincent Ferrer, priest', 'Saint Vincent Ferrer', '1350, Valencia, Spain', '1419, Vannes, France', ['builders'],
 'Vincent was a Dominican preacher who travelled across Europe calling people to repentance. Crowds of thousands followed him, and many conversions and miracles were reported.\n\nHe also worked to heal the Great Western Schism that divided the Church.'),
('04-07', 'Saint John Baptist de la Salle, priest', 'Saint John Baptist de la Salle', '1651, Reims, France', '1719, Rouen, France', ['teachers'],
 'A wealthy priest of Reims, John Baptist gave up his fortune to provide free education for poor children. He trained teachers and founded the Brothers of the Christian Schools, pioneering teaching methods still used today.\n\nHe is patron of teachers.'),
('04-11', 'Saint Stanislaus, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Stanislaus', '1030, Poland', '1079, Kraków, Poland', ['Poland'],
 'Stanislaus was Bishop of Kraków who fearlessly rebuked King Bolesław II for his cruelty and immorality. The king had him killed while he was celebrating Mass.\n\nHe is a patron of Poland.'),
('04-13', 'Saint Martin I, pope and martyr', 'Saint Martin I', '', '655, Crimea', [],
 'Pope Martin I defended the teaching that Christ has both a human and a divine will. For this the emperor had him arrested, dragged to Constantinople, humiliated and exiled to the Crimea, where he died of hardship.\n\nHe is the last pope venerated as a martyr.'),
('04-21', 'Saint Anselm of Canterbury, bishop and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Anselm of Canterbury', '1033, Aosta, Italy', '1109, Canterbury, England', [],
 'Anselm was a Benedictine abbot and later Archbishop of Canterbury who defended the freedom of the Church against the English kings. His motto, "faith seeking understanding", shaped Christian thought.\n\nHe is called the father of scholasticism and is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('04-23', 'Saint Adalbert, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Adalbert of Prague', 'c. 956, Bohemia', '997, Prussia', ['Bohemia', 'Poland'],
 'Adalbert was Bishop of Prague who worked to reform his diocese and later became a missionary. He was martyred while preaching to the pagan Prussians on the Baltic coast.'),
('04-23', 'Saint George, martyr', 'Saint George', '', 'c. 303, Lydda, Palestine', ['soldiers', 'scouts', 'England'],
 'George was a soldier martyred in Palestine during the persecution of Diocletian. The famous legend of George slaying the dragon pictures the victory of faith over evil.\n\nHe is honoured by Christians in East and West alike.'),
('04-24', 'Saint Fidelis of Sigmaringen, priest and martyr', 'Saint Fidelis of Sigmaringen', '1578, Germany', '1622, Seewis, Switzerland', ['lawyers'],
 'Fidelis was a lawyer known as "the poor man\'s lawyer" before becoming a Capuchin friar. Sent to preach in Switzerland, he was killed by opponents of the Catholic faith.'),
('04-25', 'Saint Mark the Evangelist', 'Saint Mark the Evangelist', '', '', ['notaries', 'lions'],
 'Mark, also called John Mark, travelled with Saints Paul and Barnabas and later with Saint Peter, whose preaching shaped his Gospel, the shortest of the four. His Gospel begins, "The beginning of the Good News of Jesus Christ, the Son of God."\n\nTradition says he founded the Church in Alexandria, Egypt. His symbol is the lion.'),
('04-28', 'Saint Louis Grignon de Montfort, priest', 'Saint Louis-Marie Grignion de Montfort', '1673, Montfort, France', '1716, Saint-Laurent-sur-Sèvre, France', [],
 'Louis preached missions across western France with great zeal. His book *True Devotion to Mary* teaches total consecration to Jesus through Mary, a devotion embraced by many, including Saint John Paul II.'),
('04-28', 'Saint Peter Chanel, priest and martyr', 'Saint Peter Chanel', '1803, France', '1841, Futuna, Oceania', ['Oceania'],
 'Peter Chanel was a Marist missionary who went to the island of Futuna in the Pacific. He was killed there, but within a few years the whole island had become Christian.\n\nHe is the first martyr of Oceania.'),
('04-29', 'Saint Catherine of Siena, virgin and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Catherine of Siena', '1347, Siena, Italy', '1380, Rome', ['Europe', 'Italy', 'nurses'],
 'Catherine was the 24th child of a family of Siena. A Dominican tertiary, she cared for the sick and the poor and lived a deep life of prayer. Fearless and full of love for the Church, she persuaded the Pope to return from Avignon to Rome. Her *Dialogue* records her conversations with God.\n\nShe is a Doctor of the Church and patron of Europe.'),
('04-30', 'Saint Pius V, pope', 'Saint Pius V', '1504, Italy', '1572, Rome', [],
 'Pius V was a Dominican friar who became pope after the Council of Trent and put its reforms into action, publishing the Roman Catechism and the Roman Missal.\n\nHe asked all Christians to pray the Rosary before the Battle of Lepanto in 1571.'),
('05-01', 'Saint Joseph the Worker', 'Saint Joseph the Worker', '', '', ['workers'],
 'Pope Pius XII set this feast in 1955 to honour the dignity of human work. Joseph, the carpenter of Nazareth, earned his family\'s bread with his hands, and Jesus shared that work.\n\nWe pray for all workers and for those seeking work.'),
('05-02', 'Saint Athanasius, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Athanasius', 'c. 296, Alexandria, Egypt', '373, Alexandria', [],
 'Athanasius was Bishop of Alexandria and the great defender of the faith of the Council of Nicaea that Jesus is truly God. He was exiled five times for refusing to give way to the Arians.\n\nAn African Father of the Church, he wrote the *Life of Anthony* and is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('05-03', 'Saints Philip and James, Apostles', 'Saints Philip and James, Apostles', '', '1st century', [],
 'Philip, from Bethsaida, brought Nathanael to Jesus and asked at the Last Supper, "Lord, show us the Father." James, son of Alphaeus, often called "the Less", is traditionally identified as the leader of the Church in Jerusalem and author of the Letter of James.\n\nBoth gave their lives as witnesses to Christ.'),
('05-10', 'Saint John of Avila, priest and doctor', 'Saint John of Avila', '1499, Spain', '1569, Montilla, Spain', ['Spanish clergy'],
 'John of Avila was a great preacher in southern Spain and a spiritual guide to many saints, including Teresa of Avila and John of God. He worked for the renewal of the clergy.\n\nHe was named a Doctor of the Church in 2012.'),
('05-12', 'Saint Pancras, martyr', 'Saint Pancras', '', 'c. 304, Rome', ['children'],
 'Pancras was a boy of about fourteen who was beheaded in Rome for his faith during the persecution of Diocletian.'),
('05-12', 'Saints Nereus and Achilleus, martyrs', 'Saints Nereus and Achilleus', '', 'c. 304, Rome', [],
 'Nereus and Achilleus were Roman soldiers who became Christians, left the army and were martyred. Pope Damasus wrote an inscription for their tomb.'),
('05-13', 'Our Lady of Fatima', 'Our Lady of Fatima', '', '', ['Portugal'],
 'In 1917 the Virgin Mary appeared six times to three shepherd children, Lucia, Francisco and Jacinta, at Fatima in Portugal. She asked for prayer, especially the daily Rosary, penance and conversion for peace in the world.\n\nThe apparitions ended with the "miracle of the sun" witnessed by thousands.'),
('05-14', 'Saint Matthias the Apostle', 'Saint Matthias the Apostle', '', '1st century', [],
 'After the betrayal and death of Judas, the apostles prayed and cast lots, and Matthias was chosen to complete the Twelve. He had followed Jesus from his baptism to the Resurrection.\n\nTradition says he preached and was martyred for the faith.'),
('05-18', 'Saint John I, pope and martyr', 'Saint John I', '', '526, Ravenna, Italy', [],
 'Pope John I was sent by the Arian king Theodoric to Constantinople. On his return the king, suspicious of him, imprisoned him at Ravenna, where he died of hardship.'),
('05-20', 'Saint Bernardine of Siena, priest', 'Saint Bernardine of Siena', '1380, Italy', '1444, L\'Aquila, Italy', ['advertisers', 'communications'],
 'Bernardine was a Franciscan preacher who travelled throughout Italy calling people to conversion. He spread devotion to the Holy Name of Jesus, showing the letters IHS on a tablet.'),
('05-21', 'Saint Christopher Magallanes and companions, martyrs', 'Saint Christopher Magallanes and Companions', '1869, Mexico', '1927, Mexico', ['Mexico'],
 'Christopher Magallanes was a Mexican priest killed with many companions during the persecution of the Church in Mexico in the 1920s. Before being shot he forgave his executioners.'),
('05-22', 'Saint Rita of Cascia', 'Saint Rita of Cascia', '1381, Italy', '1457, Cascia, Italy', ['impossible causes', 'difficult marriages'],
 'Rita endured a difficult marriage with patience until her husband was converted and later killed. After the deaths of her sons she entered the Augustinian convent at Cascia, where she lived in prayer.\n\nShe is invoked as patron of impossible causes.'),
('05-25', 'Saint Bede the Venerable, priest and doctor', 'Saint Bede the Venerable', 'c. 673, England', '735, Jarrow, England', ['historians'],
 'Bede spent almost his whole life in the monastery of Jarrow in England, praying, studying and teaching. His *Ecclesiastical History of the English People* earned him the title father of English history.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('05-25', 'Saint Gregory VII, pope', 'Saint Gregory VII', 'c. 1015, Italy', '1085, Salerno, Italy', [],
 'Gregory VII was a great reforming pope who fought corruption in the Church and defended its freedom from control by kings and emperors. He died in exile, saying: "I have loved justice and hated iniquity."'),
('05-25', 'Saint Mary Magdalene de Pazzi, virgin', 'Saint Mary Magdalene de\' Pazzi', '1566, Florence, Italy', '1607, Florence', [],
 'A Carmelite nun of Florence, Mary Magdalene de\' Pazzi had deep mystical experiences and bore long trials with patience. She is remembered for her love for the Church and prayer for its renewal.'),
('05-26', 'Saint Philip Neri, priest', 'Saint Philip Neri', '1515, Florence, Italy', '1595, Rome', ['Rome', 'joy', 'humour'],
 'Philip Neri, the "Apostle of Rome", drew people of all ages to God through his warmth, humour and joy. He founded the Congregation of the Oratory, whose gatherings combined prayer, Scripture, music and conversation.\n\nHe taught that cheerfulness strengthens the heart.'),
('05-27', 'Saint Augustine (Austin) of Canterbury, bishop', 'Saint Augustine of Canterbury', '', 'c. 604, Canterbury, England', ['England'],
 'Augustine was a monk sent by Pope Gregory the Great to bring the Gospel to the English. He baptised King Ethelbert of Kent and became the first Archbishop of Canterbury.'),
('05-29', 'Saint Paul VI, pope', 'Saint Paul VI', '1897, Concesio, Italy', '1978, Castel Gandolfo, Italy', [],
 'Giovanni Battista Montini guided the Second Vatican Council to its conclusion and carried out its reforms. He was the first pope to travel widely, including a visit to Uganda in 1969, the first by a pope to Africa. His encyclical *Humanae Vitae* defended the dignity of human life and love.\n\nHe was canonised in 2018.'),
('05-31', 'Visitation of the Blessed Virgin Mary', 'The Visitation of the Blessed Virgin Mary', '', '', [],
 'After the Annunciation, Mary hurried to visit her cousin Elizabeth. The child in Elizabeth\'s womb, John the Baptist, leapt for joy, and Elizabeth greeted her: "Blessed are you among women." Mary replied with her song of praise, the *Magnificat*.\n\nThe feast celebrates Mary bringing Christ to others in loving service.'),
('06-01', 'Saint Justin Martyr', 'Saint Justin Martyr', 'c. 100, Samaria', 'c. 165, Rome', ['philosophers', 'apologists'],
 'Justin was a philosopher who searched for truth in many schools until he found it in Christ. He wrote defences of the faith and the earliest description of the Mass on Sunday.\n\nHe was beheaded in Rome for refusing to sacrifice to the gods.'),
('06-02', 'Saints Marcellinus and Peter, martyrs', 'Saints Marcellinus and Peter', '', '304, Rome', [],
 'Marcellinus, a priest, and Peter, an exorcist, were martyred in Rome during the persecution of Diocletian. They are named in the Roman Canon.'),
('06-03', 'Saints Charles Lwanga and companions, martyrs', 'Saints Charles Lwanga and Companions', 'c. 1860, Buganda', '3 June 1886, Namugongo, Uganda', ['African youth', 'Catholic Action'],
 'Charles Lwanga was head of the royal pages at the court of King Mwanga of Buganda in today\'s Uganda. When the king demanded that the young Christian pages give up their faith and submit to his immoral wishes, Charles protected and encouraged them.\n\nWith twenty-one Catholic companions, many still in their teens, he was burned alive at Namugongo in 1886. Anglican martyrs died with them. Their witness sparked the rapid growth of the Church in Uganda and across Africa.'),
('06-05', 'Saint Boniface, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Boniface', 'c. 675, England', '754, Frisia', ['Germany'],
 'Boniface was an English monk who became the great missionary to the Germans. He organised the Church there and famously felled the sacred oak of Thor to show the power of the true God.\n\nHe was martyred in Frisia with his companions.'),
('06-06', 'Saint Norbert, bishop', 'Saint Norbert', 'c. 1080, Germany', '1134, Magdeburg, Germany', [],
 'After a worldly youth, Norbert experienced a dramatic conversion and became a priest and preacher. He founded the Premonstratensians, or Norbertines, and later became Archbishop of Magdeburg.'),
('06-09', 'Saint Ephrem, deacon and doctor', 'Saint Ephrem the Syrian', 'c. 306, Nisibis', '373, Edessa', [],
 'Ephrem was a deacon in Syria who wrote hundreds of hymns teaching the faith, earning the name "Harp of the Holy Spirit". He cared for the hungry during famine.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('06-11', 'Saint Barnabas the Apostle', 'Saint Barnabas the Apostle', 'Cyprus', '1st century', ['Cyprus'],
 'Barnabas, whose name means "son of encouragement", sold his field and gave the money to the apostles. He welcomed Saul after his conversion and travelled with him on his first missionary journey.\n\nTradition says he was martyred in Cyprus.'),
('06-13', 'Saint Anthony of Padua, priest and doctor', 'Saint Anthony of Padua', '1195, Lisbon, Portugal', '1231, Padua, Italy', ['lost things', 'the poor'],
 'Born in Lisbon, Anthony joined the Franciscans hoping to be a missionary in Morocco. Illness changed his plans, and in Italy his gift for preaching was discovered. His sermons converted many.\n\nHe died young and is one of the most loved saints, invoked to find lost things. He is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('06-19', 'Saint Romuald, abbot', 'Saint Romuald', 'c. 951, Ravenna, Italy', '1027, Italy', [],
 'Romuald was a monk who founded the Camaldolese order, combining community life with the solitude of hermits. He spent his life renewing monasteries.'),
('06-21', 'Saint Aloysius Gonzaga, religious', 'Saint Aloysius Gonzaga', '1568, Italy', '1591, Rome', ['young people', 'students', 'people with AIDS'],
 'Aloysius gave up his inheritance as heir of a noble family to join the Jesuits. During a plague in Rome he cared for the sick, carrying them to hospital, and caught the disease himself. He died at twenty-three.\n\nHe is patron of young people.'),
('06-22', 'Saint Paulinus of Nola, bishop', 'Saint Paulinus of Nola', 'c. 354, Bordeaux, Gaul', '431, Nola, Italy', [],
 'Paulinus was a wealthy Roman senator and poet who, with his wife Therasia, gave away his riches to the poor. He became Bishop of Nola in Italy and was known for his charity.'),
('06-22', 'Saints John Fisher, bishop and martyr and Thomas More, martyr', 'Saints John Fisher and Thomas More', '1469 and 1478, England', '1535, London', ['lawyers', 'politicians'],
 'John Fisher, Bishop of Rochester, and Thomas More, Lord Chancellor of England, refused to accept King Henry VIII as head of the Church in England. Both were beheaded in 1535.\n\nMore declared on the scaffold: "I die the king\'s good servant, but God\'s first."'),
('06-24', 'Birth of Saint John the Baptist', 'The Nativity of Saint John the Baptist', '', '', [],
 'John the Baptist is the only saint, besides Our Lady, whose birth is celebrated as a solemnity. Born to the elderly Elizabeth and Zechariah, he was sent to prepare the way for the Lord.\n\nHe pointed to Jesus, saying: "Behold the Lamb of God," and "He must increase, I must decrease."'),
('06-27', 'Saint Cyril of Alexandria, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Cyril of Alexandria', 'c. 376, Egypt', '444, Alexandria', [],
 'Cyril was Bishop of Alexandria and defended at the Council of Ephesus (431) the truth that Mary is the Mother of God, because Jesus is one divine Person.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('06-28', 'Saint Irenaeus, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Irenaeus', 'c. 130, Smyrna', 'c. 202, Lyon, Gaul', [],
 'Irenaeus learned the faith from Saint Polycarp and became Bishop of Lyon in France. His work *Against Heresies* defended the faith handed down from the apostles. He wrote: "The glory of God is man fully alive."\n\nPope Francis named him a Doctor of the Church.'),
('06-29', 'Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles', 'Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles', '', 'c. 64–67, Rome', ['the Church', 'Rome'],
 'Peter, the fisherman chosen by Jesus as the rock on which he built his Church, and Paul, the persecutor who became the Apostle to the Gentiles, both gave their lives in Rome. Peter was crucified and Paul beheaded.\n\nTogether they are the pillars of the Church.'),
('06-30', 'First Martyrs of the Church of Rome', 'The First Martyrs of the Church of Rome', '', '64, Rome', [],
 'After the great fire of Rome in 64, Emperor Nero blamed the Christians. Many were cruelly put to death in the city.\n\nThe Church honours these unnamed first martyrs the day after Saints Peter and Paul.'),
]
